{"name":"Hemisphere","hex":"#5285a4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hemisphere","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Half of the celestial sphere, as divided by either the ecliptic or the celestial equator .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}.","A realm or domain of activity ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hemisphere"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sphere","blocks":[{"text":"A sphere is a surface analogous to the circle, a curve. In solid geometry, a sphere is the set of points that are all at the same distance r from a given point in three-dimensional space. That given point is the center of the sphere, and the distance r is the sphere's radius. The earliest known mentions of spheres appear in the work of the ancient Greek mathematicians."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Sphere_wireframe_10deg_6r.svg/330px-Sphere_wireframe_10deg_6r.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sphere","wikidata":"Q12507"},{"title":"Hemispheres of Earth","blocks":[{"text":"In geography and cartography, hemispheres of Earth are any division of the globe into two equal halves (hemispheres), typically divided into northern and southern halves by the Equator and into western and eastern halves by the Prime meridian. Hemispheres can be divided geographically or culturally, or based on religion or prominent geographic features. Use of these divisions is applied when studying Earth's geographic distribution, cultural differences, and other geographic, demographic and socioeconomic features."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Primemeridian.jpg/330px-Primemeridian.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemispheres_of_Earth","wikidata":"Q399984"},{"title":"Hemisphere (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Hemisphere\"  is a song by Japanese voice actress and singer Maaya Sakamoto, released as her ninth single on February 21, 2002, by Victor Entertainment. Co-written by Yuho Iwasato and Yoko Kanno, the song served as the opening theme for the Fuji Television anime series RahXephon, which began airing on January 21, 2002."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1f/Maaya_Sakamoto_-_Hemisphere.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hemisphere_(song)","wikidata":"Q2061527"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Sphere_and_Ball.png/500px-Sphere_and_Ball.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Sphere_and_Ball.png/1280px-Sphere_and_Ball.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Sphere_and_Ball.png/500px-Sphere_and_Ball.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two orthogonal radii of a sphere","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASphere_and_Ball.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sphere_section.png/330px-Sphere_section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sphere_section.png/330px-Sphere_section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A normal vector to a sphere, a normal plane and its normal section. The curvature of the curve of intersection is the sectional curvature. For the sphere each normal section through a given point will be a circle of the same radius: the radius of the sphere. This means that every point on the sphere will be an umbilical point.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASphere_section.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Sphere_halve.png/500px-Sphere_halve.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Sphere_halve.png/500px-Sphere_halve.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Great circle on a sphere","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASphere_halve.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Loxodrome.png/500px-Loxodrome.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Loxodrome.png/500px-Loxodrome.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Loxodrome","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALoxodrome.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Einstein_gyro_gravity_probe_b.jpg/500px-Einstein_gyro_gravity_probe_b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Einstein_gyro_gravity_probe_b.jpg/960px-Einstein_gyro_gravity_probe_b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Einstein_gyro_gravity_probe_b.jpg/500px-Einstein_gyro_gravity_probe_b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEinstein_gyro_gravity_probe_b.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/King_of_spades-_spheres.jpg/250px-King_of_spades-_spheres.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/King_of_spades-_spheres.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/King_